{"version":"0.1","documentType":"opinion","source":{"kind":"lrb_html","caseId":"2024_05818","path":"/Users/jonathan/Projects/miranda/opinions/coa/2024/2024_05818.htm","publicationStatus":"published","structuredExtraction":{"opinions":"high_confidence","footnotes":"high_confidence","opinionLineCoverage":1.0,"footnoteLineCoverage":1.0,"opinionFallbackReasons":[],"footnoteFallbackReasons":["no_footnote_source_lines_detected"]},"fallback":{"headerLines":[{"lineNumber":1,"text":"People v Williams"},{"lineNumber":2,"text":"2024 NY Slip Op 05818 [42 NY3d 1049]"},{"lineNumber":3,"text":"November 21, 2024"},{"lineNumber":4,"text":"Court of Appeals"},{"lineNumber":5,"text":"Published by New York State Law Reporting Bureau pursuant to Judiciary Law § 431."}],"opinionLines":[{"lineNumber":6,"text":"{**42 NY3d at 1049} OPINION OF THE COURT"},{"lineNumber":8,"text":"{**42 NY3d at 1049} OPINION OF THE COURT"},{"lineNumber":10,"text":"Memorandum."},{"lineNumber":12,"text":"The order of the Appellate Division should be affirmed."},{"lineNumber":14,"text":"Assuming, without deciding, that the introduction of codefendant's statement was a *Bruton* error (*see Bruton v United States*, 391 US 123 [1968]), any such error was harmless. The evidence of defendant's guilt, specifically the incriminating testimony of two acquaintances who were eyewitnesses to the{**42 NY3d at 1050} crime, was overwhelming and there is no reasonable possibility that the admission of codefendant's statement affected the verdict (*see People v Cedeno*, 27 NY3d 110, 121-122 [2016]; *People v Hardy*, 4 NY3d 192, 198 [2005]; *People v Crimmins*, 36 NY2d 230, 241 [1975])."},{"lineNumber":16,"text":"Chief Judge Wilson and Judges Rivera, Garcia, Singas, Cannataro, Troutman and Halligan concur."},{"lineNumber":18,"text":"Order affirmed, in a memorandum."}],"footnoteLines":[]}},"header":{"title":"People v Williams","slipOpinion":"2024 NY Slip Op 05818","officialCitation":"42 NY3d 1049","court":"Court of Appeals","decisi